          Fragment:md
            ## Installation

            Qwik (like other JSX frameworks) uses nmbl's Vite plugin. Install it as a dev dependency:

          .md-indent
            InstallWidget(packages="@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin" dev={true})
          Fragment:md
            ## Configuration

            Add the nmbl plugin to your `vite.config.ts`. It must come **before** the Qwik plugin, and you tell it to target Qwik's JSX:

            ```ts title="vite.config.ts"
            import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
            import { qwikVite } from '@builder.io/qwik/optimizer';
            import nmbl from '@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin';

            export default defineConfig({
              plugins: [
                nmbl({ jsx: { framework: 'qwik' } }), // must come before qwikVite()
                qwikVite(),
              ],
            });
            ```

            ## Basic Usage

            Unlike Vue, Svelte, and Astro (which use a `<template lang="nmbl">` block), JSX frameworks use a **tagged template**. Import the `nmbl` tag and return ``nmbl`…` `` from your component. Qwik signals are read through `.value` inside `${…}` holes:

            ```tsx title="App.tsx"
            import { component$, useSignal } from '@builder.io/qwik';
            import { nmbl } from '@nmbl-lang/vite-plugin/tag';

            export const App = component$(() => {
              const count = useSignal(0);

              return nmbl`
                div#app
                  h1.title nmbl + Qwik
                  button(onClick$=${() => count.value++}) Clicked ${count.value} times
              `;
            });
            ```

            The plugin compiles the tagged template to Qwik JSX at build time. Interpolation uses `${expr}` holes, and Qwik's `$`-suffixed handlers (`onClick$`) pass straight through. Unlike React, Qwik keeps native HTML attribute names — `class` and `for` are preserved, not renamed.

            ## Control Flow

            nmbl's `@if` / `@elseif` / `@else` and `@each` blocks compile to the JSX control-flow expressions Qwik uses. Conditions and loop sources go in `${…}` holes (read signals with `.value` there):

            ```nmbl
            @if(${loggedIn.value})
              p Welcome back, ${user.value.name}
            @else
              a(href="/login") Log in

            ul
              @each(item of ${items.value} :key="item.id")
                li ${item.name}
            ```

            `@if(${cond})` compiles to a JSX conditional and `@each(item of ${items.value})` to a `.map()` expression — the same primitives Qwik's JSX uses. One JSX-specific rule worth knowing: when you use `:key`, the loop body must have a single root element (the key goes on it) — a multi-root `@each` body with `:key` is a compile error. nmbl's `@await` / `@key` / `@snippet` are Svelte-only and will error in the JSX target.

            ## Comments

            `//` comments are stripped from the output (dev notes); `//!` comments are rendered as real HTML comments:

            ```nmbl
            // dev note — never shipped
            //! becomes <!-- an HTML comment -->
            p Visible text
            ```

            You can also comment out individual props inside a tag's parens — handy while debugging:

            ```nmbl
            button(
              type="button"
              // disabled
              /* aria-label="Add" */
            ) Add
            ```

            ## Editor Support

            The nmbl VS Code extension highlights ``nmbl`…` `` tagged templates inside `.tsx`, `.jsx`, `.ts`, and `.js` files, so the markup reads as nmbl rather than a plain string.

          Fragment:md
            > **Note:** The nmbl language server (diagnostics, completions, go-to-definition) currently covers standalone `.nmbl` files and `<template lang="nmbl">` blocks in Svelte/Astro — not the JSX tagged-template form yet. You get syntax highlighting in Qwik files today; for the richest tooling, factor shared markup into a `.nmbl` file. Vue remains the most complete for full type intellisense via its dedicated Volar plugin.

            ## Troubleshooting

          .troubleshooting:md
            ### Tagged template not compiling

            Make sure `nmbl({ jsx: { framework: 'qwik' } })` is listed **before** `qwikVite()` in your Vite `plugins` array, and restart the dev server after editing the config.

            ### Reactivity not updating

            Qwik is signal-based — read and write signals through `.value` (`${count.value}`, not `${count}`). Reading a signal outside a `${…}` hole won't track it.

            ### Syntax errors with indentation

            nmbl uses indentation for structure. The first line of the template (inside the backticks) sets the base indent — keep it consistent (2-space steps, no tabs).

